+# 108→109→110 production CLI SIGKILL evidence [_]
+
+Authority 3.1.19 / `3ce9613262de0a8499837571137782becc01daa7b56923a53b11f7f04058f60d`. Evidence only; no production edit, acceptance checkbox, commit, or claim of full G-STAGE acceptance.
+
+## Exact executable inputs
+
+`captured-main-inputs.json` binds the complete captured src/vendor/Cargo input closure at 2026-09-12T05:00:08Z. `included-guards.json` names all three frozen cancellation guards: Chat and Anthropic already matched captured main; Gemini was explicitly overlaid from its frozen package. Core SHA `93953e4af528935ece167056e02c174632ae42e0a370e5ba61a09eeac1cdf1d9`. Vendor 77 files aggregate SHA `a873d9dd2002719bbc9e9f0904a9be02f8c1461aab26522a4cdedca3f295c750`. This does not claim equality to later main changes, including 104. `build-cli.json` records actual offline locked stable-aarch64-apple-darwin production CLI build; `binary-binding.json` binds the privately copied executable. Its exact inputs remain in `.ops/provider-kill319-current`.
+
+Before writing fixtures, relevant current provider/event/session/core owners and existing kill tests were read. Existing mock-backend process death and native protocol-error/cancel tests did not establish OS SIGKILL during real native SSE.
+
+## Actual scenarios and results
+
+Each case launches the production binary against a local TCP HTTP server using a distinct ZENPI_HOME and session. HOME/CODEX_HOME remain unchanged. The server writes native SSE, deliberately leaves the response open, and records actual HTTP bodies. After observing a public delta the fixture sends SIGKILL to that owned CLI PID, reaps exit -9 and requires socket closure. It then launches separate second and third CLI processes. All HTTP, stdout, stdin, journal/WAL before and after recovery, PIDs, exits and hashes are retained.
+
+| Provider | First SSE interrupted before finish | Completed read_file, second SSE interrupted | Actual HTTP count per successful case |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 108 Chat Completions | chat/partial-first PASS | chat/tool-corrected PASS | 2 / 3 |
+| 109 Anthropic | anthropic/partial-first PASS | anthropic/tool-first PASS | 2 / 3 |
+| 110 Gemini | google/partial-first PASS | google/tool-first PASS | 2 / 3 |
+
+The partial case contains public text and a tool delta without protocol completion. Chat/Anthropic have incomplete JSON arguments; Gemini has a complete functionCall part but no finishReason. None dispatches write_file or commits the unfinished assistant history. The completed-tool case executes actual read_file once, durably correlates call_id → operation_id → successful tool result, then kills the CLI during the next HTTP response. Native ordered signed blocks/parts survive restart exactly; one call and one result appear in new HTTP history. The interrupted second text never contaminates history.
+
+For all six cases, recovery reports exactly one provider unknown_outcome with the original operation ID and idempotency key, no unknown tool result; every observed public event is byte-exact in the durable WAL and replayed by a new process. Reusing the killed request ID returns unknown_outcome; conflicting payload returns request_id_conflict. A fresh prompt before recovery returns operation_recovery. None sends HTTP. Explicit `/recovery retry … --yes` is an idempotent durable decision only, conflicting abandon is rejected, and the killed request ID remains unknown. Only a new prompt ID makes one fresh submission and returns a clean terminal answer. Same-ID terminal replay is byte-exact within the second process and across a third process, without HTTP or tool redispatch.
+
+## Failures retained and scope
+
+`chat-first` exited 1 after the partial case passed: the original fixture wrongly required call_id on tool_execution_finished. Production associates that marker by operation_id; call_id is on tool_execution_started and the durable result. The corrected fixture verifies the complete three-way identity and passes; `kill_probe-v1.py` and original failed tool case are retained. This was not a product defect. The first offline audit also incorrectly hashed the complete WAL envelope; the owner hashes its event payload. `verify_evidence-v1.py`, failure log and corrected receipt preserve that audit correction. Six-case offline verification now passes, including input closure, binary identity, per-case artifact hashes, distinct PIDs, clean restart exits, WAL payload hashes and native identity/order checks. No behavior reruns were needed for the audit correction.
+
+The fixtures prove forced death during SSE and a known completed tool result across that boundary. They do not establish killing a command while its side effect is in progress, server-side exactly-once delivery, arbitrary process descendants, real network providers, or 111 disk write fault handling. No unresolved product failure was found in these six scenarios. Full mandated suites and product acceptance remain with main.
